<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 4: Cleaning and Recoding Data                            -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens with the West Africa Ebola outbreak (2014-2016), drawing on -->
<!--  two real, verified papers:                                       -->
<!--    - Owada et al. (2016), Frontiers in Public Health 4:163           -->
<!--      (Sierra Leone national surveillance data problems)            -->
<!--    - Roshania et al. (2016), Global Health: Science and Practice   -->
<!--      4(3), 394-409 (IMC's multicountry clinical data collection)   -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-cleaning-recoding" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Cleaning and Recoding Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-cleaning-recoding">
    <p>
      In the previous chapter, we explored the Kenya Road Accidents database and
      noticed problems along the way: county names spelled two different ways,
      and ages given in groups as well as numerically.
      We noted them and moved on.
      This chapter is about what to do with them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Data cleaning is the work of making a dataset ready for analysis.
      This can be by fixing inconsistencies, handling missing values, recoding
      variables into more useful forms, and checking that what the data says it
      contains is actually what it contains.
      It is easy to underestimate how much it matters.
      Decisions you make while cleaning include what counts as a duplicate, what
      an absent value means, and how to group a free-text description.
      These decisions shape every result that follows.
      Skip them, rush them, or hand them to software without supervision, and
      you can produce a confident, precisely wrong answer.
    </p>

    <p>
      That happened, at the largest possible scale, during the deadliest
      outbreak of Ebola virus disease ever recorded.
      We begin there.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Ebola, West Africa 2014-2016                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-ebola-data">
    <title>A Preventable Disaster</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        From 2014 to 2016, an outbreak of Ebola virus disease swept through
        Guinea, Liberia, and Sierra Leone.
        There were more than 11,000 reported deaths — the largest Ebola epidemic
        ever recorded.
      </p>

      <p>
        Fighting an epidemic this size depends on knowing, as close to real time
        as possible, where cases are appearing, how fast they are growing, and
        who each infected person has been in contact with.
        All of that depends on data.
        Two independent accounts, written by people who worked inside the
        response, describe how that data was managed at both the national level
        and in individual treatment centres.
        One of these accounts is very explicit about the consequence: the state
        of the data management system
        <q>may have 
        contributed to the magnitude and long duration of the outbreak.</q>
        This is a story about how failing to clean and reconcile data through
        duplicate records, inconsistent values, and incomplete data may have
        helped an epidemic run longer and grow larger, resulting in over 11,000
        deaths.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-two-systems">
      <title>Two systems, neither reliable on its own</title>

      <p>
        Owada et al.
        (2016) describe a national data management system built from two
        parallel tools that were never properly reconciled with each other: The
        Epi Info VHF line list database, and an Excel-based line list.
      </p>

      <p>
        Every case was meant to be recorded on a paper "case investigation form"
        (CIF).
        This was to be filled in in the field, and then subsequently entered
        into the <term>Epi Info VHF line
        list database</term>, a tool
        built by the US Centers for Disease Control specifically for outbreak
        case and contact-tracing data.
        In principle, it supported multiple people entering data at once and
        merging district databases into a national one.
        In practice, most district data managers were not experienced users of
        the software, duplicate records were genuinely difficult to detect and
        merge once created, and CIFs routinely arrived late at the district
        response centres.
        This meant the VHF database was never fully up to date, even late in the
        outbreak.
      </p>

      <p>
        To add, laboratory results and case investigation data were often
        entered <em>independently</em> of each other instead of the lab result
        being added to the existing associated case record.
        This meant that the same case could end up as two separate database
        entries, with no link to combine them together.
      </p>

      <p>
        As well as the Epi Info database, district teams also kept a second,
        entirely separate <term>Excel-based line list</term>.
        This was built mainly from laboratory sample results and contained a
        subset of the CIF variables like age, sex, location, and the dates a
        sample was collected, received, and tested.
        The district teams found that this was quicker to produce reports and
        visualise the situation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-collection">
      <title>Incomplete at the source</title>

      <p>
        Even when the systems worked as designed, the data was often incomplete.
        Many CIF records had no date of onset, sex, age, and/or residence.
        These are all gaps that the authors attribute to inadequate training and
        supervision of surveillance officers under outbreak conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        In addition, the number of deaths from EVD were hard to pin down.
        Confirming a death was EVD-related required a post-mortem mouth swab,
        which was often not taken in areas that continued traditional burial
        practices.
        This meant that the count of <em>confirmed</em> EVD deaths likely
        understated the true number.
        In the other direction, before the outbreak, Sierra Leone was already
        dealing with malaria severe enough to account for roughly 38% of
        hospital admissions and about 30% of under-five deaths.
        Importantly, malaria shares clinical features with EVD.
        That overlap plausibly delayed recognition of genuine EVD cases, and
        risked malaria deaths being misclassified as EVD deaths.
        The same underlying data problem of incomplete, hard-to-verify records
        pushed the death count in both directions at once, for different
        reasons, in different places.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-transmission">
      <title>Lost between the form and the file</title>

      <p>
        The intended process was that two copies of the original CIF would be
        produced for every death, and three copies for every suspected living
        case: one to accompany the sample to the laboratory, one to the district
        response centre, and for suspected cases, one to stay with the patient.
        In the early period of the outbreak this process did not function
        effectively.
        Reporting of deaths from holding centres, community care centres and
        treatment centres had no clear, structured process until daily phone
        calls were later introduced, and burial teams, treatment centres, and
        laboratories used inconsistent definitions when counting deaths in the
        first place.
        A meaningful amount of information was simply never transmitted anywhere
        it could be used.
      </p>

      <p>
        What did arrive still had to be compiled, and there was no consolidated
        database that captured and linked every data source in a structured way.
        Each district response centre had adopted its own method for recording
        cases, and inconsistency in the Excel line-list format across districts
        caused ongoing problems merging and aggregating cases into national
        totals.
        In the authors' own words,
        <q>much of the data has never found its way into any structured
        database and may never be recoverable.</q>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-cfr">
      <title>A number that was biased whichever way you calculated it</title>

      <p>
        The clearest illustration of what this cost is the
        <term>case fatality rate</term> (CFR).
        CFR is the confirmed deaths divided by confirmed cases.
        This basic number in an outbreak helps track both how dangerous the
        disease is, and how contained it is.
      </p>

      <p>
        Undercounting deaths means that the CFR calculation was biased downward,
        and the severity of the situation is underestimated.
        Further, if you restrict the calculation to cases with a known final
        outcome (alive or dead), then a new bias is introduced: <q>alive</q>
        could only be recorded once a patient's hospitalisation had fully
        concluded, while a death could be recorded as soon as it happened.
        Cases still awaiting an outcome were therefore more likely, at any given
        moment, to eventually resolve as deaths than the data so far suggested,
        which biases <em>this</em> version of the CFR upward instead.
        Neither calculation was wrong in the sense of containing an arithmetic
        error, but both were systematically biased, in opposite directions, by
        exactly the same underlying data problems.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-ebola-cfr-lesson">
        <title>No clean number was hiding underneath</title>

        <p>
          There was no clean version of this number sitting underneath the messy
          data.
          The data management failures described above added noise around a true
          value, but also changed which direction the bias in the headline
          statistic ran.
          The system meant to hold the full picture could not be trusted to give
          one, so analysts instead used laboratory data for day-to-day situation
          reports for the rest of the outbreak.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        The authors are explicit that this was never fully resolved: a complete
        cleaning and reconciliation of all case data was never finalised.
        Even at the end of the outbreak, a large number of suspected cases
        remained permanently unclassified - neither confirmed as EVD nor ruled
        out.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-clinical">
      <title>The same failure, one level down</title>

      <p>
        Roshania et al.
        (2016) tell a parallel story from inside the treatment centres
        themselves.
        International Medical Corps ran five Ebola Treatment Units across
        Liberia and Sierra Leone, treating more than 2,500 patients and amassing
        over 25,000 pages of clinical, epidemiological, and operational data
        over the course of the epidemic.
      </p>

      <p>
        Even a single, well-resourced organisation running its own units under
        its own protocols could not keep that data consistent.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Data-entry was not consistent.
              It varied between treatment units and, within a treatment unit.
              This made it difficult to track the severity of the epidemic.
              More patients meant more data to enter, and staff availability did
              not scale up to match.
              The moment data quality was most likely to slip was at the peak of
              the outbreak <mdash/> exactly the moment when accurate data
              mattered most.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Paper forms were filled out inside the high-risk treatment zone.
              However, these forms physically degraded from repeated chlorine
              disinfection.
              Getting the data out safely meant reading forms through a fence
              and copying them by hand, photographing them across a barrier, or
              sending someone in full protective equipment purely to photograph
              paperwork with a waterproof camera.
              Every additional step introduced opportunities for transcription
              errors, typos, missing values, or misread information.
              This gives an additional challenge of ensuring that the data
              remained accurate as it moved from the original form into a usable
              dataset.
              Small errors introduced during the transfer could easily become
              part of the analysis and ultimately affect the decisions made from
              the data.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Some variables, such as recent bush meat consumption or travel
              history, were recorded in some forms and not others, making a
              combined dataset impossible without deciding what to do about the
              gap.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The same clinical sign was written down under different names in
              different places ( <q>red injected eyes,</q>
              <q>conjunctivitis,</q> <q>hemorrhagic eyes</q> ) and had to be
              manually recoded into a single consistent variable before it meant
              anything as data.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        An initial quality audit was performed where a random sample of patient records
        was compared field by field against the original scanned charts. The audit 
        found error rates high enough that the organisation had to re-enter
        large sections of the dataset by hand.
        Even then, resources were limited enough that a choice had to be made
        about what to re-enter: triage data was redone for every patient, but
        the more detailed daily rounding and treatment data was only redone for
        patients confirmed EVD-positive.
        After that effort, they reached roughly 99% accuracy.
        Getting there took a dedicated data-cleaning team working
        <em>after the fact</em>, months later, on data collected under some of
        the most dangerous conditions imaginable.
      </p>

      <p>
        The organisation's own retrospective judgement is blunt:
        <q>much
        time and effort could have been saved through closer oversight and
        real-time quality assurance assessments in the field.</q>
        The cleaning was not the problem.
        Doing all of it afterward, once the epidemic had already moved on, was.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-ebola-lesson">
        <title>Cleaning was not optional overhead here</title>

        <p>
          In both accounts, the failure to standardise, deduplicate, and
          consolidate case data was not a data-management inconvenience sitting
          alongside the real work of fighting the epidemic.
          It <em>was</em> the epidemic response, failing at one of its most
          basic tasks: knowing, accurately, what was happening.
          When you cannot calculate a case fatality rate or reliably merge two
          districts' records, you cannot see the outbreak clearly enough to
          fight it well.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Owada, K., Eckmanns, T., Kamara, K.O.B.
        and Olu, O.O.
        (2016).
        <q>Epidemiological Data Management during an
        Outbreak of Ebola Virus Disease: Key Issues and Observations from
        Sierra Leone.</q>
        <em>Frontiers in Public Health</em>, 4, 163.
        Roshania, R.
        et al.
        (2016).
        <q>Successful Implementation of a Multicountry Clinical Surveillance
        and Data Collection System for Ebola Virus Disease in West Africa:
        Findings and Lessons Learned.</q>
        <em>Global Health: Science and
        Practice</em>, 4(3), 394 <ndash/>
        409.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 2: the STACK question that exemplifies this              -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-stack-crop-example">
    <title>STACK Question Here</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The scale is nowhere near comparable, but the underlying mistake in the
        Ebola data is deciding, without quite deciding, what anomalies in the
        data actually means. This shows up constantly at far smaller scale.
        Before you can ask a question of a dataset, you need to know whether the
        data is actually ready to be asked.
        The following exercise asks you to do exactly that.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch4-stack">
      <title>A survey of 100 farmers</title>

      <p>
        A survey was conducted with 100 farmers.
        The dataset contains three variables: <c>Age</c> (the age of the
        farmer), <c>Variety</c> (the crop variety they planted), and
        <c>Yield</c> (their harvest yield).
        The goal is to understand whether younger or older farmers tend to plant
        different varieties, and whether that affects their yields.
      </p>

      <p>
        But before we can ask that question, we need to make sure the data is
        ready for analysis.
        Download the dataset in the STACK question, and work through the
        following questions.
        You will find that some of them have more than one defensible answer,
        and that the answer changes depending on decisions you make about the
        data first.
      </p>

      <p>
        TODO: insert STACK question
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: it happened again, elsewhere                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-other-disasters">
    <title>When the data looks fine but isn't</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The Ebola story involved many simultaneous failures; for example,
        duplicate records, inconsistent formats, data lost in transmission,
        missing values, and using the wrong tools for the job.
        But, nothing in the output announced that anything was wrong.
        The two stories that follow share that same structure: a pipeline that
        appeared to be working, producing results that looked legitimate, while
        something was quietly broken underneath.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-phe-pipeline">
      <title>
        COVID-19 in England: the ceiling nobody was watching out for
      </title>

      <p>
        During the COVID-19 pandemic, many countries ran contact tracing
        programmes: when someone tested positive, public health workers would
        contact everyone that person had been in close contact with, and ask
        them to isolate.
        The faster a positive case was notified and their contacts traced, the
        fewer people they were likely to have infected in the meantime.
      </p>

      <p>
        In England, positive test results from testing labs were sent out as CSV
        files.
        Those files were imported into a template built on the pre-2007 Excel
        <c>.xls</c> format, and forwarded on to contact tracers.
        This had been working successfully for several months.
      </p>

      <p>
        In late September 2020, the reported daily case counts were lower than
        other indicators suggested they should be.
        But, the files were still coming in, and contact tracers were still
        working through the files.
        From the outside, the system looked like it was doing its job - just
        with slightly better numbers than expected, during a period when the
        outbreak was in fact growing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Public Health England (PHE), the government body responsible for public
        health in England, had set up an automated process to pull CSV files
        from commercial testing labs into Excel templates, which were then
        forwarded to the NHS Test and Trace team.
        The problem lay in the file format.
        The developers had built the templates using the pre-2007 <c>.xls</c>
        format, which has a hard limit of 65,536 rows per sheet.
        Because each test result occupied several rows once the template columns
        were filled in, each template could hold only around 1,400 cases.
        When a batch of results exceeded that limit, the cases beyond it were
        simply left off the file.
        No error was raised and the template looked complete — but the overflow
        cases never reached contact tracers.
        This lasted for eight days in total.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is why the numbers had looked manageable.
        They were not lower because the outbreak was slowing — they were lower
        because the outbreak was growing above the 1400 limit.
        Every time a daily batch exceeded 1,400 cases, the excess was being
        silently cut off before it reached anyone who could act on it.
        Between 25 September and 2 October 2020, 15,841 positive test results
        were lost this way.
        The people behind those results were not notified in time, and the
        people they had been in contact with were not traced and told to
        isolate, during a period when the outbreak in England was actively
        growing.
      </p>

      <p>
        The fix, once the problem was identified, was straightforward: split the
        incoming files into smaller batches so that no single template exceeded
        the row limit.
        The health secretary at the time noted that the underlying system had
        already been scheduled for replacement since July, it just had not been
        replaced yet.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-phe-lesson">
        <title>The failure was silence, not error</title>

        <p>
          A crash would have been noticed immediately.
          A file that simply stops growing, with no warning and no error
          message, looks exactly like a file that succeeded.
          Checking that the number of rows you exported matches the number of
          rows you expected is one of the cheapest habits in data cleaning, and
          this is exactly what it exists to catch.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> UK Government,
        <q>Slides to accompany coronavirus 
  press conference, CMO, 31 October 2020.</q>
        Leo Kelion,
        <q>Excel: Why 
  using Microsoft's tool caused Covid-19 results to be lost,</q>
        BBC, 5 October 2020,
        <url href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/technology-54423988"/>;
        <url href="https://www.theregister.com/2020/10/05/excel_england_coronavirus_contact_error/" visual="The Register, 5 October 2020"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-gene-names">
      <title>Gene names: recoding you didn't ask for</title>

      <p>
        The previous example is about data silently disappearing.
        This one is about data quietly turning into something else.
        And this happened, unnoticed, across an entire scientific field, for
        more than a decade.
      </p>

      <p>
        We have around 20,000 human genes.
        To ensure clear scientific communication and consistent database
        tracking, each gene is given a short alphanumeric symbol; for example:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Metastasis Associated Lung Adenocarcinoma Transcript 1</q> is
              given the symbol <c>MALAT1</c>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Septin 2</q> is given the symbol <c>SEPT2</c>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Membrane Associated Ring-CH-Type Finger 1</q> is given the
              symbol <c>MARCH1</c>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        Genomics researchers routinely list hundreds of these symbols in
        spreadsheets as part of their published research.
        Microsoft Excel, with its default settings, tries to be helpful.
        If a cell's contents look like a date, then Excel silently converts it,
        storing it in a new date format instead of how it was initially
        inserted.
        For example, <c>SEPT2</c> looks enough like <q>2 September</q> for Excel
        to interpret it as a date and silently convert it to <c>2-Sep</c>.
        Similarly, <c>MARCH1</c> becomes <c>1-Mar</c>.
        In total, there are 27 different genes that Excel has been found to
        mistake for dates.
        The issue is that with Excel, it does not give a warning.
        Instead, it silently replaces the gene with a date, thinking it is being
        helpful, even though this has nothing to do with the biology.
      </p>

      <p>
        In 2016, researchers Mark Ziemann, Yotam Eren and Assam El-Osta screened
        3,597 papers published between 2005 and 2015 in 18 leading genomics
        journals.
        These papers were all published, peer-reviewed research in leading
        journals, hence the kind of work other researchers assume as a reliable
        foundation.
        Roughly <term>one in five</term> of the papers that included a
        supplementary Excel gene list had at least one gene name silently
        corrupted into a date or a number.
      </p>

      <p>
        So, why does this matter?
        A gene symbol is more than a label: it is the key that links a finding
        in one paper to the same gene in every other paper, database, and
        analysis tool that uses it.
        When a researcher downloads a supplementary file to build on someone
        else's work, they might search for <c>SEPT2</c> and find nothing,
        because the file contains <c>2-Sep</c> instead.
        Or worse, they might not notice the corruption at all, carry the wrong
        value forward into their own analysis, and publish results that are
        built on a gene list that was silently wrong from the start.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two things are worth noting.
        First, nobody had decided to recode <c>SEPT2</c> into a date — the
        software had done it for them, and it had gone unnoticed for years at a
        time, across an entire discipline.
        Second, this was not a new problem.
        The error had been documented with workarounds suggested as early as
        2004 — over a decade before Ziemann et al.'s investigation.
        The knowledge of the problem existed, and a fix had been outlined.
        However, the corruption continued regardless.
      </p>

      <p>
        By 2020 the problem was serious enough that the HUGO Gene Nomenclature
        Committee (HGNC), the international body responsible for approving and
        standardising human gene names, renamed twenty-seven genes specifically
        to stop this from happening.
        For example, <c>MARCH1</c> became <c>MARCHF1</c>; <c>SEPT1</c> became
        <c>SEPTIN1</c>.
        The genes themselves had not changed, but their names were changed
        because it was easier to rename twenty-seven genes than to get Excel to
        stop guessing.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-genes-lesson">
        <title>Recoding is not always something you choose to do</title>

        <p>
          Every time you import a spreadsheet, a piece of software has already
          made decisions about how to interpret every cell — what looks like a
          number, what looks like a date, what looks like text — before you have
          looked at any of it yourself.
          Cleaning data is not just fixing values that are obviously wrong.
          It includes checking that nothing was silently <q>fixed</q> for you,
          incorrectly.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Ziemann, M., Eren, Y.
        and El-Osta, A.
        (2016).
        <q>Gene name errors are widespread in the scientific 
      literature.</q>
        <em>Genome Biology</em>, 17, 177;
        <url href="https://www.genenames.org" visual="genenames.org"/> (HGNC,
        2020 renaming guidelines).
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 5: applied to the flamingo data                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-kenya-accidents-r-cleaning">
    <title>Cleaning the Kenya Road Accidents Data in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>, we loaded the Kenya Road Accidents
        database and began exploring it using pivot tables, summaries, and
        simple plots.
        In doing so, we noticed several problems: inconsistent county names,
        ambiguous time values, mixed gender entries, and free-text accident
        descriptions that needed grouping before they could be analysed.
        This section works through those problems one by one, showing how data
        cleaning and exploration feed into each other — each round of cleaning
        revealing something new worth looking at, and each new look revealing
        something else that needs fixing.
      </p>

      <p>
        We use the same dataset and the same packages as before.
        If you have not already loaded them:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      library(rpivotTable)
      library(readxl)
      library(dplyr)
      library(stringr)
      library(lubridate)
      library(ggplot2)
      library(purrr)
      library(tidyr)

      kenya_accidents_database &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        One small confession before we start: it was not until I opened this
        dataset in Excel that I realised the 13th column was simply a note
        someone had typed into the spreadsheet, not data at all.
        I had a suspicion from <c>View()</c> in R, but I did not fully recognise
        it until I could see the whole sheet laid out visually.
        This is worth saying: R is powerful, but it does not show you your data
        the way a spreadsheet does.
        Using different tools together (e.g., R for analysis, Excel or a
        spreadsheet viewer for a visual sanity check) is a good habit to have.
        Different software have different strengths.
      </p>

      <p>
        With that established, the first thing to do is remove that 13th column:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      names(kenya_accidents_database)
      kenya_accidents_database$`...13` &lt;- NULL
        </code>
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-time">
      <title>Making sense of <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c></title>

      <p>
        Let's start with the <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> column.
        This is worth looking at carefully before doing anything with it.
        There are several ways to inspect it.
        Three such suggestions are:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Option 1: pivot table
      rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database, rows = "TIME 24 HOURS")

      # Option 2: unique values
      unique(kenya_accidents_database$`TIME 24 HOURS`)

      # Option 3: counts of each value
      kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
        group_by(`TIME 24 HOURS`) %&gt;%
        count() %&gt;%
        View()
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Whichever method you use, a few things stand out immediately.
      </p>

      <p>
        First, there are missing values.
        In the pivot table these appear as a column labelled <c>NULL</c>- that
        is how <c>rpivotTable</c> displays <c>NA</c> 's.
        But in the <c>unique</c> option or the count table they appear as
        <c>NA</c> directly.
        Either way, we can see that we are missing some values for the time of
        the crash.
      </p>

      <p>
        Second, there is an entry <c>UNKNOWN TIME</c>.
        This is not a missing value in the technical sense since it is text that
        someone typed into the cell, but, it is clearly meant to convey the same
        thing: the time was not known.
        We can confidently treat it as missing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Third, and most interestingly, there are times that look wrong.
        Most entries are four digits ( <c>1030</c>, <c>1130</c>, <c>1915</c> )
        which is what you would expect for 24-hour time.
        But some entries have only three digits ( <c>140</c>, <c>630</c> ), two
        digits ( <c>10</c>, <c>45</c> ), or even one digit ( <c>1</c> ).
        What do these mean?
        Could these be typos where <c>140</c> means <c>1400</c> or <c>0140</c>?
      </p>

      <p>
        The most likely explanation is Excel.
        When you type a number beginning with <c>0</c> into an Excel cell
        formatted as a number, Excel silently removes the leading zero.
        So <c>0140</c> becomes <c>140</c>, <c>0010</c> becomes <c>10</c>, and
        <c>0001</c> becomes <c>1</c>.
        Under this interpretation, any entry with fewer than four digits has
        simply had its leading zeros stripped, and we can restore them by
        padding to four digits with leading zeros.
        The <c>NA</c> entries might then represent <c>0000</c> (midnight)
        similarly stripped to nothing.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is an educated guess, not a certainty.
        In a real investigation, you would contact the data custodian to
        confirm.
        But, for the purposes of this example, we will assume we did exactly
        that, and received confirmation that yes, the leading zeros were
        stripped by Excel, exactly as in the gene names story from earlier in
        this chapter.
        With that confirmed, we can fix the column:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
        mutate(
          # Treat "UNKNOWN TIME" as NA
          `TIME 24 HOURS` = na_if(`TIME 24 HOURS`, "UNKNOWN TIME"),

          # Pad to 4 digits with leading zeros
          # e.g. "140" -&gt; "0140", "10" -&gt; "0010", NA -&gt; "0000"
          `TIME 24 HOURS` = case_when(
            is.na(`TIME 24 HOURS`) ~ "0000",
            TRUE ~ str_pad(`TIME 24 HOURS`, width = 4, 
                           side = "left", pad = "0")
          ),

          # Convert to a proper time type (hhmm format)
          TIME_PARSED = hm(paste0(
            str_sub(`TIME 24 HOURS`, 1, 2), ":",
            str_sub(`TIME 24 HOURS`, 3, 4)
          ))
        )
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Before moving on, it is worth checking that the conversion worked as
        expected.
        We want to confirm that each value of <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> maps to
        exactly one <c>TIME_PARSED</c> value with no surprises:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
      group_by(`TIME 24 HOURS`, TIME_PARSED) %&gt;%
      count() %&gt;%
      View()
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Each value of <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> should have exactly one corresponding
        <c>TIME_PARSED</c> entry.
        If any had two or more, that would mean the padding or parsing had done
        something unexpected and we would need to investigate.
        Assuming everything looks correct, we can move on.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can now verify the result by checking the unique values of
        <c>TIME_PARSED</c>, and plot the distribution of crashes by hour to see
        whether it looks sensible:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
        mutate(HOUR = hour(TIME_PARSED))

      ggplot(kenya_accidents_database, aes(x = HOUR)) +
        geom_bar() +
        labs(title = "Crashes by hour of day",
             x = "Hour (24-hour clock)",
             y = "Number of incidents")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The distribution should show a peak in the evening hours, consistent
        with what Muguro et al (2020) found in the five-year dataset, and with
        what we saw in the hourly chart from their paper earlier in this
        chapter.
        If the pattern looks broadly right, that is a reasonable sanity check
        that the padding assumption was correct.
        If it looked wildly wrong (for example, a spike at 3am with nothing
        during the day) that would be a signal to go back and question the
        assumption.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4_img1_crashes">
        <caption>
          Crashes by hour of day, Kenya Road Accidents database, April <ndash/>
          June 2016.
          Crashes peak between 19:00 and 21:00, consistent with evening rush
          hour.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_img1_crashes.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart showing the number of fatal road accidents by hour of
              the day on the 24-hour clock.
              Counts are generally low between 2-5am, then rise gradually
              through the morning and afternoon.
              Counts rise sharply from hour 18 onwards, peaking at around 46 at
              hour 20, then declining through hours 21 and 22 before rising
              slightly again at hour 23.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Note that the <c>NA</c> s we assigned to <c>0000</c> will now appear as
        midnight crashes.
        If you are doing any analysis of time-of-day patterns, it is worth
        either excluding them or flagging them separately, since they are
        imputed values rather than confirmed times.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-county">
      <title>Fixing county names</title>

      <p>
        The time column had implausible values - entries that needed
        interpretation before they could be used.
        A different kind of problem appears in <c>COUNTY</c>: not values that
        are wrong, but values that are inconsistent.
        The same county, spelled two different ways, will be treated as two
        different counties by any analysis you run.
        Opening a pivot table on <c>COUNTY</c> makes these easy to spot, since
        the names are listed alphabetically and duplicates appear next to each
        other:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database, rows = "COUNTY")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Two problems are visible straight away: <c>KERCHO</c> and <c>KERICHO</c>
        appear as separate entries, as do <c>MURANGA</c> and <c>MURANG'A</c>.
        These are clearly the same counties entered inconsistently (see left
        image in <xref ref="fig-ch4-counties-1"/> ).
        We fix them with <c>case_when()</c>:
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-counties-1">
        <caption>
          Pivot table of <c>COUNTY</c> counts before (left) and after (right)
          cleaning.
          The left panel shows <c>KERCHO</c> and <c>KERICHO</c> as separate
          entries (highlighted in orange), and <c>MURANG'A</c> and
          <c>MURANGA</c> as separate entries (also highlighted).
          The right panel shows these have been merged into single entries after
          cleaning.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_img2_counties_1.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two side-by-side pivot tables showing county counts.
              The left table, representing the data before cleaning, has orange
              highlighting around two pairs of duplicate entries: KERCHO with 1
              incident and KERICHO with 5 incidents; and MURANG'A with 3
              incidents and MURANGA with 3 incidents.
              The right table, representing the data after cleaning, shows
              KERCHO merged into KERICHO with 6 total incidents, and MURANGA
              merged into MURANG'A with 6 total incidents.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(COUNTY = case_when(
            COUNTY == "KERCHO"  ~ "KERICHO",
            COUNTY == "MURANGA" ~ "MURANG'A",
            TRUE ~ COUNTY
          ))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Verify by pulling up the pivot table again: the duplicates should now be
        gone.
        It is worth doing this check explicitly rather than assuming the fix
        worked.
        (See right image in <xref ref="fig-ch4-counties-1"/> ).
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database, rows = "COUNTY")
        </code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-base">
      <title>Fixing base/sub-base names</title>

      <p>
        The same problem appears in the <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> column — spelling
        variants for the same location, and some entries prefixed with
        <c>DTEO</c>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-ch4_img2_base"/>.
        We believe this refers to the District Traffic Enforcement Officer
        responsible for that area, making <c>DTEO DAGORETTI</c> equivalent to
        <c>DAGORETTI</c> — but in practice, you would want to verify this with
        someone familiar with the data before stripping it, in case the prefix
        carries meaning that matters for your analysis.
        For the purposes of this example, we proceed on the assumption that it
        does not:
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4_img2_base">
        <caption>
          Frequency of Base/Sub-base values in the data (shown via pivot table).
          Several entries refer to the same location under different spellings —
          for example, <c>DAGORETTI</c>, <c>DAGORETI</c>, and
          <c>DTEO DAGORETTI</c> all appear as separate entries and need to be
          standardised before analysis.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_img2_base.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A pivot table listing Base/Sub-base values alphabetically with
              their counts.
              Several spelling variants of the same location are visible as
              separate entries, including DAGORETI, DAGORETTI, and DTEO
              DAGORETTI.
              Other variants visible include MOIS BRIDGE and MOI'S BRIDGE, and
              SALGA and SALGAA.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(
            `BASE/SUB BASE` = str_trim(`BASE/SUB BASE`),
            `BASE/SUB BASE` = str_remove(`BASE/SUB BASE`, 
                                          regex("^DTEO\\s+", ignore_case = TRUE)),
            `BASE/SUB BASE` = case_when(
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "DAGORETTI"   ~ "DAGORETI",
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "MOI'S BRIDGE" ~ "MOIS BRIDGE",
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "MAKINGENI"   ~ "MAKONGENI",
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "MURANGA"     ~ "MURANG'A",
              `BASE/SUB BASE` == "SALGAA"      ~ "SALGA",
              TRUE ~ `BASE/SUB BASE`
            )
          )
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        We can now use the relationship between <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> and
        <c>COUNTY</c> as a further quality check.
        A county can have multiple sub-bases, but, we may expect a sub-base to
        belong to one county only, so any sub-base that appears in two different
        counties may signal that something is wrong.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database,
                    rows = "BASE/SUB BASE",
                    cols = "COUNTY")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-counties-2">
        <caption>
          Cross-tabulation of Base/Sub-base against County (selected columns
          shown).
          This view reveals two problems.
          First, NAKURU base has 12 total incidents: 11 in Nakuru county and 1
          in <c>MAKURU</c> — almost certainly a typo.
          Second, NYANDO base has 4 incidents recorded in Homa Bay and 4 in
          Kisumu.
          A quick lookup confirms that Nyando is in Kisumu county — but it is
          worth asking whether the Homa Bay entries are a typo, or whether there
          is a separate place with a similar name in Homa Bay that was also
          recorded as NYANDO.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_img2_counties_2.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A pivot table cross-tabulating Base/Sub-base values in rows
              against County in columns, with counts in each cell.
              Only selected county columns are shown.
              Key observations visible in the table: NAKURU base has 11
              incidents in Nakuru county and 1 in Makuru county, totalling 12;
              Embakasi base has 11 incidents all in Nairobi county; Nyando base
              has 4 incidents in Homa Bay and 4 in Kisumu, totalling 8; Makindu
              base has 8 incidents all in Makueni county; Starehe base has 8
              incidents all in Nairobi county.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Sorting by count, we can see that <c>NAKURU</c> has 12 cases: 11 in
        Nakuru county and 1 in <c>MAKURU</c>.
        This is almost certainly a typo, and we fix it the same way as before.
        This is a good example of how cleaning one variable reveals a problem in
        another.
        We were not looking at <c>COUNTY</c> at this point — we were looking at
        <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> — but the cross-tabulation surfaced it.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(COUNTY = case_when(
            COUNTY == "MAKURU" ~ "NAKURU",
            TRUE ~ COUNTY
          ))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        We also notice that <c>NYANDO</c> appears in both Homa Bay and Kisumu
        counties.
        A quick check confirms that Nyando is in Kisumu county, so the Homa Bay
        entries are likely errors — but it is worth going back to the original
        source before correcting.
        Could it instead be that <c>NYANDO</c> is the error, and Homa Bay is the
        correct county?
        The data alone cannot tell us.
        This is exactly the kind of question that needs a domain expert, or
        access to the original records, rather than a statistical fix.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-dates">
      <title>Dates: converting and checking</title>

      <p>
        We next look to the <c>Date DD/MM/YYYY</c> column.
        In R, this needs to be converted from text to a proper date type before
        we can do anything useful with it.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(`Date DD/MM/YYYY` = as.Date(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Once converted, a simple bar chart reveals something unexpected:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(kenya_accidents_database,
               aes(x = `Date DD/MM/YYYY`)) +
          geom_bar()
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-dates">
        <caption>
          Number of fatal road accidents per day, Kenya Road Accidents database,
          April <ndash/> June 2016.
          Note the complete absence of data in May, suggesting this month was
          not included in the extract rather than that no crashes occurred.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_dates.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart showing the count of fatal road accidents for each
              date in the dataset.
              Data is present for April and June but entirely absent for May,
              leaving a clear gap in the middle of the plot.
              Within April, there is a notable spike of 19 incidents on one day
              in mid-April, with most other days recording between 3 and 12
              incidents.
              The data in April appears to end around late April with two bars
              close together near the end of the month before the May gap.
              June data begins in early June and runs to approximately the 26th,
              with counts generally between 3 and 12 per day and a spike of
              around 12 in mid-June.
              Both April and June show gaps of zero incidents on certain dates,
              most notably towards the end of each month.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        There are no crashes in May — the data jumps from April directly to
        June.
        This is almost certainly not because no crashes occurred in May, but
        because May data was simply not included in this extract.
        This is not something we can fix, but it is important to know: any
        analysis of monthly patterns from this dataset will be misleading,
        because the dataset is not a complete three-month record.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can check this Date column by also looking at patterns by day of the
        week.
        To do this, we extract the weekday from the date:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(weekday = lubridate::wday(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`, label = TRUE))

        ggplot(kenya_accidents_database,
               aes(x = weekday)) +
          geom_bar()
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-weekday">
        <caption>
          Number of fatal road accidents by day of week, Kenya Road Accidents
          database, April <ndash/> June 2016.
          Saturday has by far the highest count, followed by Friday, while
          Monday has the fewest.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_weekday.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart showing the total count of fatal road accidents for
              each day of the week.
              Saturday has the highest count at just over 100, followed by
              Friday at around 70.
              Sunday and Wednesday are roughly equal at around 48 each.
              Tuesday and Thursday are similar at around 42 and 38 respectively.
              Monday has the lowest count at approximately 28.
              The pattern shows a clear increase towards the end of the week,
              peaking on Saturday.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Fridays and Saturdays have the most crashes, and Mondays the fewest.
        This is broadly consistent with what Kenya's National Road Safety Action
        Plan reports — nearly half of all fatal crashes occur between Friday and
        Sunday.
        But it is worth noting that reporting delays could also play a role:
        crashes that happen late on a given day might be logged the following
        day, which could shift some of the apparent Friday peak into Saturday.
        We also notice gaps on the 27th and 28th of both April and June — two
        consecutive days with no crashes recorded in both months, which seems
        unlikely and warrants checking against the original data source.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-individual">
      <title>Moving to the individual level (Advanced)</title>

      <p>
        Some variables in the dataset belong at the level of the incident; for
        example, the road, the time, the county, the cause.
        However, other variables in this data set belong at the level of the
        individual victim: their age, gender, and category.
        But the data as downloaded is at the incident level, with multiple
        victims sometimes encoded in a single row (See
        <xref ref="table-kenya-accidents-snippet"/> which shows a snippet of the
        data.).
        As we discussed in <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>, mixing levels makes
        analysis harder and can introduce errors.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-kenya-accidents-snippet">
        <title>
          A snippet of rows from the Kenya Road Accidents database after initial
          cleaning (Note that `NO.` is the number of individuals involved in the
          crash)
        </title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell/>
            <cell>TIME 24 HOURS</cell>
            <cell>BASE/SUB BASE</cell>
            <cell>COUNTY</cell>
            <cell>ROAD</cell>
            <cell>PLACE</cell>
            <cell>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</cell>
            <cell>GENDER</cell>
            <cell>AGE</cell>
            <cell>CAUSE CODE</cell>
            <cell>VICTIM</cell>
            <cell>NO.</cell>
            <cell>Date DD/MM/YYYY</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>66</cell>
            <cell>2015</cell>
            <cell>Bureti</cell>
            <cell>Kericho</cell>
            <cell>Buret Ngoina</cell>
            <cell>Ngoina Junction</cell>
            <cell>
              The Probox was overtaking unknown M/V hence collided head on with
              the petrol tanker
            </cell>
            <cell>M</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>7 Passengers 1 Driver</cell>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>2016-06-16</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>224</cell>
            <cell>2020</cell>
            <cell>Vihiga</cell>
            <cell>Vihiga</cell>
            <cell>Chavakali Kapsabet</cell>
            <cell>Musasa Area</cell>
            <cell>Head on collision</cell>
            <cell>M</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>8</cell>
            <cell>Passenger</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>2016-04-21</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>327</cell>
            <cell>2205</cell>
            <cell>Nanyuki</cell>
            <cell>NA</cell>
            <cell>Kinamba</cell>
            <cell>Ngarua</cell>
            <cell>The vehicle overturned killing 6 victims</cell>
            <cell>4F &amp; INF</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>26</cell>
            <cell>Passenger</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>2016-04-08</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>86</cell>
            <cell>1920</cell>
            <cell>Kangundo</cell>
            <cell>Makueni</cell>
            <cell>Kangundo Nairobi</cell>
            <cell>Kalaah Kware Bridge</cell>
            <cell>The vehicle lost control and veered off the road</cell>
            <cell>2M &amp; 2F</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>26</cell>
            <cell>4 Passengers 1 Driver</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>2016-06-12</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>117</cell>
            <cell>1800</cell>
            <cell>Makueni</cell>
            <cell>Makueni</cell>
            <cell>Wote Makindu</cell>
            <cell>Kisene Bridge</cell>
            <cell>
              The vehicle lost control and plunged into the Miseke Bridge
            </cell>
            <cell>M</cell>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>26</cell>
            <cell>Passenger</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>2016-06-08</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The cleaner approach is to create two linked data frames: one at the
        incident level, and one at the individual level.
        The reason this matters comes down to what each variable actually
        describes.
        Some variables belong to the crash itself (the road, the time, the
        county, the cause code, the date), but these are the same for every
        person involved in a given incident.
        Other variables belong to the individual victim (their age, gender, and
        category), and these differ from person to person within the same crash.
      </p>

      <p>
        Keeping everything in one data frame creates problems in both
        directions.
        If we want to convert <c>AGE</c> to a numeric variable, R will turn
        entries like <c>"2M &amp; 2F"</c> in <c>GENDER</c> into <c>NA</c>, but
        those are not missing values, they are multiple people.
        On the other hand, if we expand each row so that every person gets their
        own row, then crash-level variables like <c>ROAD</c> and
        <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> get duplicated across every victim of the same
        crash, which makes counting crashes correctly much harder.
        The solution is to separate the two levels, link them with a shared ID,
        and work with each at the right level of analysis — exactly as we
        discussed in <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>.
        We start by adding an incident ID so that once the levels are separated,
        we can link them back together:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(incident_id = 1:nrow(kenya_accidents_database))

        kenya_individual_level &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          dplyr::select(incident_id, AGE, GENDER, `NO.`, VICTIM)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The <c>GENDER</c> column contains entries like <c>"2M &amp; 2F"</c> or
        <c>"4F &amp; INF"</c> — multiple people encoded in a single cell.
        To get one row per person, we need to expand these.
        This kind of string parsing is fiddly enough that using a helper
        function (or asking an AI tool to draft one and then checking it
        carefully) is a reasonable approach:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # Helper to split entries separated by commas, "&amp;", or "and"
        split_flexible &lt;- function(x) {
          str_split(x, "\\s*(?:[,&amp;]|and)\\s*")
        }

        kenya_individual_level &lt;- kenya_individual_level %&gt;%
          mutate(
            AGE    = split_flexible(AGE),
            GENDER = split_flexible(GENDER),
            VICTIM = split_flexible(VICTIM)
          ) %&gt;%
          mutate(
            AGE    = map2(AGE,    `NO.`, rep_len),
            GENDER = map2(GENDER, `NO.`, rep_len),
            VICTIM = map2(VICTIM, `NO.`, rep_len)
          ) %&gt;%
          unnest(cols = c(AGE, GENDER, VICTIM))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Note that this expansion cannot tell us which gender matches which age
        when both are mixed in the same incident — we only know that a given
        incident involved, say, two men and two women, not which of the four
        victims was which.
        This is a limitation of the original data, not of the cleaning.
        For incidents where <c>NO.</c> (the number of victims) is greater than
        one and the victim types are mixed, the safest approach is to handle
        them manually — there are only a handful of such cases in this dataset,
        so it is feasible.
        The key constraint throughout is not to inadvertently link an age or
        gender to the wrong victim type in the process.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>AGE</c> column contains a mix of numeric ages, the letter
        <c>A</c> (adult), the letter <c>J</c> (junior), and entries like
        <c>"5 MONTHS"</c>.
        We clean these in two steps: first converting month-based ages to
        decimal years, then creating a categorical <c>AGE_GROUP</c> variable
        that preserves the <c>A</c> and <c>J</c> classifications:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # Convert "5 MONTHS" to 0.42, etc.
        clean_months &lt;- function(df, column_name, format = "decimal") {
          col &lt;- sym(column_name)
          pattern &lt;- "(?i)(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)\\s*(?:months?|mth|mths)"
          df %&gt;%
            mutate(
              .months = as.numeric(str_match(!!col, pattern)[, 2]),
              !!col := case_when(
                !is.na(.months) &amp; format == "decimal" ~
                  as.character(round(.months / 12, 2)),
                TRUE ~ as.character(!!col)
              )
            ) %&gt;%
            select(-.months)
        }

        kenya_individual_level &lt;- clean_months(kenya_individual_level, 
                                               "AGE", format = "decimal")

        # Create AGE_GROUP from A/J codes and numeric ages
        kenya_individual_level &lt;- kenya_individual_level %&gt;%
          mutate(
            .age_num = suppressWarnings(as.numeric(AGE)),
            AGE_GROUP = case_when(
              AGE %in% c("A", "ADULT")    ~ "ADULT",
              AGE %in% c("J", "JUNIOR")   ~ "JUNIOR",
              !is.na(.age_num) &amp; .age_num &gt;= 18 ~ "ADULT",
              !is.na(.age_num) &amp; .age_num &lt;  18 ~ "JUNIOR",
              TRUE ~ NA_character_
            )
          ) %&gt;%
          select(-.age_num)

        # Now convert AGE to numeric (A, J become NA - that is expected)
        kenya_individual_level$AGE &lt;- as.numeric(kenya_individual_level$AGE)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        We can check that <c>AGE_GROUP</c> was created correctly by
        cross-tabulating it against <c>AGE</c> in a pivot table:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    rpivotTable(kenya_individual_level, rows = "AGE_GROUP", cols = "AGE")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The result, shown in <xref ref="fig-ch4-agegroup-by-age"/>, confirms
        that the grouping has worked as expected.
        All numeric ages below 18 appear in the <c>JUNIOR</c> row, and all ages
        18 and above appear in the <c>ADULT</c> row.
        The <c>null</c> row (where neither group was assigned) corresponds to
        the rows where <c>AGE</c> was recorded only as <c>A</c> or <c>J</c> and
        could not be converted to a number, which is exactly what we would
        expect.
        Nothing has been lost and nothing has been misclassified.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-agegroup-by-age">
        <caption>
          Cross-tabulation of <c>AGE_GROUP</c> against numeric <c>AGE</c>,
          confirming that the grouping was applied correctly.
          All ages below 18 fall in the <c>JUNIOR</c> row and all ages 18 and
          above fall in the <c>ADULT</c> row.
          The <c>null</c> row is empty, indicating no cases were left ungrouped
          among those with a numeric age.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_agegroup_by_age.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A pivot table with AGE_GROUP in rows and numeric AGE values as
              column headers.
              The ADULT row contains counts only for ages 18 and above, with
              values such as 3 at age 18, 5 at age 19, and increasing counts at
              ages 25, 30, and 35.
              The JUNIOR row contains counts only for ages below 18, including
              ages 0.25, 0.42, 0.75, 1.5, and whole numbers from 3 to 17.
              The null row is empty throughout.
              The Totals row matches the sum of ADULT and JUNIOR for each age,
              confirming no observations were missed or miscategorised.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        We can also check the numeric age conversion by plotting a barplot of
        the numeric ages variable:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    ggplot(kenya_individual_level, aes(x = AGE)) +
      geom_bar()
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The plot in <xref ref="fig-ch4-age-bar"/> shows a striking pattern:
        there are large spikes at ages 30, 35, and 40.
        There are also spikes at 50, 60, and 70 relative to the ages around it.
        At first glance it might look like road crash victims in Kenya are
        unusually likely to be exactly 30 or exactly 35 years old.
        The more plausible explanation is simpler: people recording the data
        were rounding ages to the nearest 5 or 10, rather than recording exact
        ages.
        This is a common pattern in self-reported or administratively recorded
        age data, and it is a good example of data that is not wrong exactly,
        but is not as precise as it appears.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-age-bar">
        <caption>
          Distribution of victim ages in the Kenya Road Accidents database,
          April <ndash/> June 2016.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_age_bar.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart showing the count of road accident victims by age.
              Pronounced spikes are visible at ages 30, 35, 40, 50, 60, and 70,
              consistent with age rounding in the original data.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This is one reason why <c>AGE_GROUP</c>, which only distinguishes adult
        from junior, may actually be more honest for this dataset than treating
        <c>AGE</c> as a precise numeric variable.
        That said, collapsing everyone over 18 into a single <q>adult</q>
        category also loses real information — a 19-year-old and a 70-year-old
        are very different road users.
        A reasonable middle ground would be broader age bands such as 18
        <ndash/> 30, 31 <ndash/> 40, and so on, which preserve meaningful
        variation without implying a precision that was never there.
        The right choice depends on what question you are trying to answer.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can also look at the age variable with a quick summary and boxplot:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        summary(kenya_individual_level$AGE)
        boxplot(kenya_individual_level$AGE)
        
        #&gt;    Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.    NA's 
        #&gt;    0.25   22.00   30.00   31.41   40.00   90.00     229
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Ages range from 0.25 (3 months) to 90 years, with a median of 30 years.
        The minimum and maximum look plausible.
        The 229 missing values are expected — they are the rows where age was
        recorded only as <c>A</c> or <c>J</c>, which cannot be converted to a
        number without making assumptions we are not entitled to make.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-accident-category">
      <title>Recoding accident descriptions</title>

      <p>
        The <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</c> column contains 148 distinct free-text
        descriptions.
        Many of them describe the same type of event (e.g., head-on collision,
        hit and run, lost control) written differently each time.
        To make this column useful for analysis, we need to recode it into a
        smaller set of categories.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the kind of task where Generative AI tools can genuinely help —
        not to make the decisions for you, but to draft the initial
        categorisation which you then check and correct.
        The code below was produced that way: AI suggested the initial
        groupings, and then they were reviewed manually against the actual
        entries.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(ACCIDENT_CATEGORY = case_when(

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("HEAD ON|HEAD-ON", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Head on collision",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("HIT.*R(A|U)N|HIT &amp; R(A|U)N", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Hit and run",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("LOST CONTROL|LOSE CONTROL|TYRE BURST|STEERING ROD|POWER FAIL",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Lost control",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("KNOCKED DOWN|KNOCK DOWN|RAN OVER|RUN OVER|KNOCKED PEDESTRIAN",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Knocked down",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("REAR|FROM BEHIND|FAILED TO KEEP DISTANCE",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Rear collision",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("HIT.*M/CYCLE|HIT.*MOTOR CYCLE|HIT.*CYCLIST|HIT.*CYCLE",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Vehicle hit motorcycle/cyclist",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("OVERTOOK|OVERTAKING|OVERTAK",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Overtaking incident",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("ROLL|OVERTURN", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Rolled/overturned",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("VEERED OFF|VEER OFF|LANDED INTO A DITCH|LANDED IN A DITCH|PLUNGED|PLUGGED INTO",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Veered off road",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("ALIGHT|ALIGHTED|JUMPED OFF|FELL DOWN AND WAS",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Alighting/boarding incident",

            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("RAMMED INTO|RAMMED", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Vehicle rammed into another",

            TRUE ~ "Other/unknown"
          ))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Reviewing the result with a pivot table — with both
        <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> and <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</c> in the rows —
        shows a handful of miscategorisations that need correcting.
        For example,
        <c>"THE VEHICLE OVERTOOK A M/CYCLE AND LOST CONTROL 
      VEERING OFF THE ROAD &amp; ROLLED"</c>
        was assigned to Lost control, but is better described as an overtaking
        incident.
        And <c>"THE MOTOR CYCLE HIT THE UNKNOWN M/V LOOSING CONTROL"</c> (note
        the typo) was not caught by the lost control pattern.
        We fix these in a second pass, using <c>TRUE ~ ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> at
        the end to preserve all previous assignments and only override the
        specific cases we want to change:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_database &lt;- kenya_accidents_database %&gt;%
          mutate(ACCIDENT_CATEGORY = case_when(

            # Overtaking takes priority over lost control
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("OVERTOOK|OVERTAKING|OVERTAK", 
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Overtaking incident",

            # Veered off road + knocked down stays in veered off road
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("VEERED OFF|VEER OFF", ignore_case = TRUE)) &amp;
              str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                         regex("KNOCKED DOWN|KNOCK DOWN", 
                               ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Veered off road",

            # Catches "LOOSING CONTROL" typo
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("LOST CONTROL|LOSE CONTROL|LOOSING CONTROL|LOSING CONTROL|
                              TYRE BURST|STEERING ROD|POWER FAIL",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Lost control",

            # Collided with goes to head on collision
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("COLLIDED WITH", ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Head on collision",

            # Knocked down, excluding cycle knocking down a vehicle
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("KNOCKED DOWN|KNOCK DOWN|RAN OVER|RUN OVER",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) &amp;
              !str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                          regex("CYCLE KNOCKED DOWN THE VEHICLE", 
                                ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Knocked down",

            # Rammed, including cycle knocking down a vehicle
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("RAMMED|CYCLE KNOCKED DOWN THE VEHICLE",
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Vehicle rammed into another",

            # Fell and was run over goes to other
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("FELL DOWN AND WAS OVER RAN", 
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Other/unknown",

            # Hit a pedestrian goes to knocked down
            str_detect(`BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS`,
                       regex("HIT.*PEDESTRIAN|HIT A PEDESTRIAN", 
                             ignore_case = TRUE)) ~ 
              "Knocked down",

            # Keep everything else as previously assigned
            TRUE ~ ACCIDENT_CATEGORY
          ))
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The order of conditions matters here, just as it did in the first pass.
        <c>case_when()</c> applies the first matching rule and stops, so putting
        overtaking before lost control means that a crash involving both words
        gets assigned to overtaking, which is the more specific and informative
        category.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can check the result by pulling up the pivot table again, with both
        <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> and <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</c> in the rows:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_database, 
                rows = c("ACCIDENT_CATEGORY", "BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS"))
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-accidents-check">
        <caption>
          Pivot table showing accident categories alongside the original brief
          accident descriptions, allowing manual verification of the
          categorisation.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_accidents_check.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A pivot table showing accident categories in the left column
              alongside the original brief accident descriptions in the middle
              column, with total counts on the right.
              Categories shown include: Alighting/boarding incident (4 entries,
              e.g.
              "The victim alighted while the vehicle was in motion"); Head on
              collision (63 entries across 10 descriptions, dominated by "HEAD
              ON COLLISION" with 51 incidents); Hit and run (54 entries across 4
              descriptions, dominated by "HIT AND RUN" with 46 incidents); and
              Knocked down (partial, showing 16 descriptions including
              pedestrians, cyclists, and motorcyclists being knocked down by
              various vehicles).
              The table illustrates how the free-text descriptions have been
              grouped into consistent categories, while also revealing remaining
              spelling variants within categories such as "HIT AND RAN" and "HIT
              &amp; RAN" alongside "HIT AND RUN".
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Reviewing the result, you may find entries you would have categorised
        differently — and someone else might then disagree with <em>your</em>
        choice.
        Recoding free text into categories is inherently a judgement call: there
        is rarely one objectively correct answer, only choices that are more or
        less defensible and more or less useful for the analysis at hand.
        What matters is that the choices are made deliberately and
        transparently, and that the original <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</c>
        column has not been overwritten — so if you or a collaborator disagrees
        with a categorisation, the original text is still there to go back to.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-payoff">
      <title>What the cleaning unlocks</title>

      <p>
        With <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> and <c>weekday</c> now available, both
        variables we created through data cleaning, we can start asking
        questions that would have not been possible with the raw data.
        For example, is one type of accident more likely on a certain day of the
        week?
        This kind of question does two things at once: it is genuinely
        interesting analytically, and it also serves as a check on the date data
        itself.
        If weekday patterns look completely random — no difference at all
        between a Monday and a Saturday — that might be a signal that something
        went wrong in how dates were recorded or converted.
        A plausible pattern, on the other hand, gives us some confidence that
        the dates are broadly correct.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(kenya_accidents_database,
               aes(x = weekday, fill = ACCIDENT_CATEGORY)) +
          geom_bar(position = "fill") +
          labs(y = "Proportion", x = "Day of week",
               fill = "Accident category")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-bar-accident-weekday">
        <caption>
          Proportion of each accident category by day of week, Kenya Road
          Accidents database, April <ndash/> June 2016.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_bar_accident_weekday.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A stacked proportional bar chart with day of week on the x-axis
              and proportion on the y-axis.
              Each bar is divided into twelve accident categories shown in
              different colours.
              Hit and run (olive green) and knocked down (bright green) dominate
              most days.
              Vehicle rammed into another (pink) appears as a notably larger
              proportion on Saturdays compared to weekdays.
              Head on collision (orange) is consistently present across all
              days.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The figure shows the proportion of each accident category by day of
        week.
        Hit and run and knocked down dominate across all days, but there are
        some differences worth noting.
        Vehicle rammed into another appears as a larger proportion of Saturday
        crashes than weekday crashes — which might be consistent with drink
        driving, though the dataset is too small to draw firm conclusions.
        We can also look at it the other way around, with accident category on
        the x-axis and day of week as the fill:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    ggplot(kenya_accidents_database,
           aes(x = ACCIDENT_CATEGORY, fill = weekday)) +
      geom_bar(position = "fill") +
      coord_flip() +
      labs(y = "Proportion", x = "Accident category",
           fill = "Day of week")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch4-bar-accident-weekday-2">
        <caption>
          Proportion of each day of week within each accident category, Kenya
          Road Accidents database, April <ndash/> June 2016.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_bar_accident_weekday_2.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A stacked proportional bar chart with accident category on the
              x-axis and proportion on the y-axis.
              Each bar is divided into seven days of the week shown in a colour
              scale from dark purple (Sunday) to yellow (Saturday).
              Saturday (yellow) makes up a notably large proportion of the
              Vehicle rammed into another and Lost control categories.
              Overtaking incidents appear relatively more common on Thursdays
              and Fridays.
              Most categories show a broadly even spread across weekdays.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This view makes it easier to see which categories are disproportionately
        weekend or weekday events.
        Overtaking incidents, for example, appear more concentrated on Thursdays
        and Fridays — possibly reflecting higher traffic volumes at the end of
        the working week.
        Again, at this stage, these patterns should be treated as exploratory
        observations rather than firm conclusions.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cleaning-conclusion">
      <title>Summary</title>

      <p>
        Notice that both <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> and <c>weekday</c> were
        created during cleaning.
        The exploration we can now do is only possible because of the cleaning
        that preceded it.
        And it goes the other way too — the exploration motivated the cleaning.
        It was only by plotting the date distribution that we noticed May was
        missing.
        It was only by looking at the accident descriptions in a pivot table
        that we saw how many variants needed collapsing.
        Cleaning and exploration are not two separate stages with a clear
        boundary between them.
        They are a cycle, and you move back and forth between them throughout an
        analysis.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can keep going with this data.
        For example, looking at <c>NO.</c>, the number of victims per incident,
        against <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c> reveals some useful flags: An
        alighting/boarding incident with only one victim makes sense.
        A hit and run with two victims is plausible.
        These checks do not necessarily reveal errors, but they surface cases
        worth a second look — and that is exactly what validation is for.
      </p>

      <p>
        To close, it is worth summarising the different kinds of cleaning we did
        in this chapter, because they represent distinct problems that come up
        repeatedly in real data:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-cleaning-approaches">
        <title>Types of data cleaning and examples from this chapter</title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Type of problem</cell>
            <cell>What to look for</cell>
            <cell>Example from this chapter</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Implausible values</cell>
            <cell>
              Values that cannot be correct given what the variable represents
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> entries with 1, 2, or 3 digits instead of 4;
              leading zeros stripped by Excel
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Inconsistent values</cell>
            <cell>The same thing recorded in different ways</cell>
            <cell>
              <c>KERCHO</c> vs <c>KERICHO</c>; <c>HIT AND RAN</c> vs
              <c>HIT AND RUN</c>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Missing values</cell>
            <cell>Values that are absent or encoded as text placeholders</cell>
            <cell>
              <c>UNKNOWN TIME</c> in <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c>; <c>NA</c> s in county
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Wrong level of analysis</cell>
            <cell>Multiple observations encoded in a single row</cell>
            <cell>
              <c>"2M &amp; 2F"</c> in <c>GENDER</c>;
              <c>"7 PASSENGERS 1 DRIVER"</c> in <c>VICTIM</c>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Recoding free text</cell>
            <cell>
              Unstructured descriptions that need grouping before analysis
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <c>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</c> collapsed into
              <c>ACCIDENT_CATEGORY</c>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Wrong data type</cell>
            <cell>
              Variables stored as the wrong type for what they represent
            </cell>
            <cell>
              Dates stored as text; ages stored as characters including codes
              like <c>A</c> and <c>J</c>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Silent transformation</cell>
            <cell>Values changed by software without warning</cell>
            <cell>
              Leading zeros stripped from time values by Excel; gene names
              converted to dates
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        One final point: several of the decisions in this chapter required going
        beyond the data itself to validate a choice.
        We looked up whether Nyando is in Kisumu or Homa Bay county.
        We assumed the leading-zero explanation for the time values and noted
        that in a real investigation we would confirm this with the data
        custodian.
        This kind of external validation (checking against a map, a published
        source, a domain expert, or the original data collector) is a normal and
        necessary part of data cleaning.
        Sometimes you have to go beyond the data to find out if a value is
        wrong.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-cleaning-recoding">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <!-- Conceptual: Ebola -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-ebola-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-ebola-two-systems"/> describes case data being entered
          independently in two places (laboratory records and case investigation
          forms), with no process forcing the two together.
          Suggest one concrete check, applied at the point of data entry rather
          than afterwards, that could have caught a duplicate case before it
          reached a national situation report.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- Conceptual: PHE -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-phe-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The PHE failure described in <xref ref="ss-phe-pipeline"/> was
          dangerous specifically because it was silent: the file that lost data
          looked exactly like a file that had not.
          Suggest one concrete check that, if run automatically every time a
          file was produced, would have caught this problem within a day rather
          than a week.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- Conceptual: gene names -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-gene-names">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The gene name corruption described in <xref ref="sec-gene-names"/> was
          documented with workarounds suggested as early as 2004, yet continued
          undetected across thousands of papers for over a decade.
          Suggest two things that could have been done differently — one at the
          level of individual researchers, and one at the level of journal
          editors or peer reviewers — that might have caught or prevented the
          problem earlier.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: inconsistent values -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-county-names">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following exercises use the Kenya Road Accidents database
          introduced in this chapter.
          Download it from
          <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database">data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database</url>
          and load it into R before starting.
        </p>

        <p>
          Load the Kenya Road Accidents database and inspect the <c>COUNTY</c>
          column.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use <c>sort(unique(kenya_accidents_database$COUNTY))</c> to list
              all unique county values.
              How many distinct values are there?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Identify at least two pairs of values that appear to refer to the
              same county but are recorded inconsistently.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Write R code using <c>case_when()</c> to standardise those values,
              and verify the result by running <c>sort(unique(...))</c> again.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: implausible values -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-time-values">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Inspect the <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> column in the Kenya Road Accidents
          database.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              How many distinct values does the column contain?
              Use <c>group_by()</c> and <c>count()</c> to list them with their
              frequencies.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Identify at least three values that appear implausible or
              ambiguous, and explain what you think each one might actually
              represent.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Apply the leading-zero padding fix described in this chapter and
              convert the result to an hour variable using <c>lubridate</c>.
              Plot the distribution of crashes by hour and describe what you
              see.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: missing values -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-missing-values">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The Kenya Road Accidents database contains missing values in several
          columns.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Write R code to count the number of missing values in each column.
              Which column has the most missing values?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The <c>AGE</c> column has both true missing values and coded
              values like <c>A</c> and <c>J</c>.
              After converting <c>AGE</c> to numeric, how many <c>NA</c> s
              result?
              Are all of these genuinely unknown ages, or are some of them
              something else?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Explain in your own words the difference between a value that is
              missing and a value that is unknown.
              Does that distinction matter for this dataset?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: recoding -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-victim-recode">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>VICTIM</c> column in the Kenya Road Accidents database contains
          inconsistent entries — for example, <c>M/CYCLIST</c>,
          <c>MOTOR CYCLIST</c>, and <c>MOTORCYCLIST</c> all appear as separate
          categories.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use <c>sort(unique(kenya_accidents_database$VICTIM))</c> to list
              all distinct victim categories.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Write R code using <c>case_when()</c> and <c>str_detect()</c> to
              recode the <c>VICTIM</c> column into a smaller set of clean
              categories.
              State clearly what categories you chose and why.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              After recoding, use <c>count()</c> to summarise the distribution
              of victim categories.
              Does the pattern match what Muguro et al.
              found in the five-year dataset?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: levels of analysis -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-individual-level">
      <statement>
        <p>
          This chapter created a separate individual-level data frame from the
          incident-level Kenya Road Accidents database.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              In your own words, explain why keeping age and gender in the
              incident-level data frame creates problems for analysis.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              After expanding the data to the individual level using the code
              from this chapter, how many rows does
              <c>kenya_individual_level</c> have?
              Why is this different from the number of rows in
              <c>kenya_accidents_database</c>?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Using the individual-level data frame, produce a bar chart of
              <c>GENDER</c> and describe what you see.
              Are there any categories that suggest further cleaning is needed?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <!-- Conceptual: cleaning and exploration cycle -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-cycle">
      <statement>
        <p>
          This chapter argues that data cleaning and data exploration are not
          two separate stages but a cycle — each informing the other.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Give one example from this chapter where exploration revealed
              something that needed cleaning.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Give one example where cleaning made a new kind of exploration
              possible that was not possible before.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              In the Kenya Road Accidents data, the <c>CAUSE CODE</c> column
              contains numeric codes whose meaning is only partially documented.
              Describe one way you might use exploration to try to work out what
              the undocumented codes mean, without access to the original
              codebook.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>